(2043668, 281549, 31794, '<td valign="top">almost beautiful... the horizon is tilting, and that is enough to destroy you image, and to make things worse.. you placed the horizon in the center of the image..<br/> <br/>but I''m not the kind that only says it''s bad, this could be beautiful, so in case you are not very good with photoshop, but still have it..<br/> <br/>first open the picture, then zoom out so it doesn''t fill your screen, then press CTRL-T (transform) then you get points in every corner and in the center of each outline, place the pointer at one corner, the pointer should turn to an arrow that is bent in a corner, click the mousebutton and hold it down ,drag the corner to one side until the horizon is straight. then press Enter. then crop the image, and either cut of the top or of the bottom so the horizon gets close to one third from top or bottom.<br/> <br/>that should make your image much much better.<br/> <br/>just my 2¢</td>', 'almost beautiful... the horizon is tilting, and that is enough to destroy you image, and to make things worse.. you placed the horizon in the center of the image.. but I''m not the kind that only says it''s bad, this could be beautiful, so in case you are not very good with photoshop, but still have it.. first open the picture, then zoom out so it doesn''t fill your screen, then press CTRL-T (transform) then you get points in every corner and in the center of each outline, place the pointer at one corner, the pointer should turn to an arrow that is bent in a corner, click the mousebutton and hold it down ,drag the corner to one side until the horizon is straight. then press Enter. then crop the image, and either cut of the top or of the bottom so the horizon gets close to one third from top or bottom. that should make your image much much better. just my 2¢', '2006-01-16 15:43:59.000000', NULL, 1),
