(2113367, 278826, 49007, '<td valign="top">**** Greeting from the Critique Club ****<br/> <br/>Compostion<br/>- Good or Bad? How can it be fixed? The composition is okay, but you missed an opportunity to get a better reflection of the temple in the puddle in the foreground. With the hazy/cloudy weather you really have to squint to see the temples which make this site famous. I do like the leading lines of the stones and path. There are mixed emotions about the blurry guy, and I share those mixed feeling. I usually don''t like Blurred people in scenes, but this one doesn''t bug me.<br/>- Is there anything that should be removed? The top third of the image. Lose the overexposed sky and the temple stands out.<br/>- Good use of Depth of Field? Yes.<br/>- Good focus? The issues that commentors are having don''t having to do with the focus so much as the length of the exposure. Bump it up to ISO 200 or 400 and you''ll get much better reactions because your people won''t be out-of-focus.<br/> <br/>Lighting <br/>- Good use of light? The lighting is questionable. The clouds are blown out, but the details along the edges of the path are lost.<br/> <br/>Aesthetics/Artistic Appeal:<br/>- What is my reaction or feelings? I share the opinion that the viewpoint is powerful in this image. My eye keeps going into those clouds though. You may have intentionally or unintentionally wanted them in the image, but they don''t help it.</td>', '**** Greeting from the Critique Club **** Compostion- Good or Bad? How can it be fixed? The composition is okay, but you missed an opportunity to get a better reflection of the temple in the puddle in the foreground. With the hazy/cloudy weather you really have to squint to see the temples which make this site famous. I do like the leading lines of the stones and path. There are mixed emotions about the blurry guy, and I share those mixed feeling. I usually don''t like Blurred people in scenes, but this one doesn''t bug me.- Is there anything that should be removed? The top third of the image. Lose the overexposed sky and the temple stands out.- Good use of Depth of Field? Yes.- Good focus? The issues that commentors are having don''t having to do with the focus so much as the length of the exposure. Bump it up to ISO 200 or 400 and you''ll get much better reactions because your people won''t be out-of-focus. Lighting - Good use of light? The lighting is questionable. The clouds are blown out, but the details along the edges of the path are lost. Aesthetics/Artistic Appeal:- What is my reaction or feelings? I share the opinion that the viewpoint is powerful in this image. My eye keeps going into those clouds though. You may have intentionally or unintentionally wanted them in the image, but they don''t help it.', '2006-02-02 01:10:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2113383, 281597, 5885, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Greetings from Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, as requested, here is an indepth critique of your submission.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Beautiful photography, definitely worthy of the Best of 2006 challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>I see the rule of thirds all over the place here.  This is a very strong composition with points of interest all around the photo. It''s like you could walk, well perhaps swim, into the scene and investigate.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour, focus, and light):</b> <br/>Focus - sharp, very nice.<br/>Colour - your saturations are wonderful in the sky, but the landscape could use a bit more saturation.<br/>Light - I can''t find a region in this photo that I consider poorly exposed. There is detail everywhere. <br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>I don''t like the frame, but that''s just personal taste and probably doesn''t affect your vote much. But, I really don''t know how you could have improved this shot. Perhaps, more dramatic lighting? Landscapes aren''t my strong point, so anything from me would be just guessing on working on bettering this photo.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>I think it is a wonderful photo, would do well in the postcard biz. I''m a little suprised it didn''t score higher. Was a tough challenge though.<br/> <br/>Hope to see more from you soon,<br/>Leroy</td>', '::: Greetings from Critique Club :::  Hi, as requested, here is an indepth critique of your submission. First Impression - the most important one: Beautiful photography, definitely worthy of the Best of 2006 challenge. Composition: I see the rule of thirds all over the place here.  This is a very strong composition with points of interest all around the photo. It''s like you could walk, well perhaps swim, into the scene and investigate. Technical (Colour, focus, and light): Focus - sharp, very nice.Colour - your saturations are wonderful in the sky, but the landscape could use a bit more saturation.Light - I can''t find a region in this photo that I consider poorly exposed. There is detail everywhere.  To grow its vote?: I don''t like the frame, but that''s just personal taste and probably doesn''t affect your vote much. But, I really don''t know how you could have improved this shot. Perhaps, more dramatic lighting? Landscapes aren''t my strong point, so anything from me would be just guessing on working on bettering this photo. Summary: I think it is a wonderful photo, would do well in the postcard biz. I''m a little suprised it didn''t score higher. Was a tough challenge though. Hope to see more from you soon,Leroy', '2006-02-02 01:17:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(2113576, 280112, 41337, '<td valign="top">What happened here? Were people THAT put off by the unique desaturation? And what is this complaint about the photo "not telling a story"? If this had some hot chick instead of child NOBODY would be complaining they didn''t see a "story".<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-02 03:50:41.</i></td>', 'What happened here? Were people THAT put off by the unique desaturation? And what is this complaint about the photo "not telling a story"? If this had some hot chick instead of child NOBODY would be complaining they didn''t see a "story".', '2006-02-02 03:49:32.000000', '2006-02-02 03:50:41.000000', 0),
