(2125015, 277383, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Welcome to DPC!  I see this is your fourth image and each time you have scored better.  Keep up the good work;  you''ll end up on the front page soon, at this rate.<br/> <br/>I love the composition of this image.  I''m delighted by the expressions on the faces of each of the players.  LIke many of your commentors, I am puzzled by the difference in skin tone from front to back.  And I desperately wish there was slightly better focus but with an aperture of 1.6 you got exactly what you exposed for.  I can only think that a higher ISO rating might have made a difference.  But I don''t think I am telling you anything new.<br/> <br/>But, still, this is an exciting image and it did score well. (I''ll be in Paris towards summer, and know I''ll never find a scene this lovely)<br/> <br/>IN any event,  I''ll look forward to seeing more of your images on DPC.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Welcome to DPC!  I see this is your fourth image and each time you have scored better.  Keep up the good work;  you''ll end up on the front page soon, at this rate. I love the composition of this image.  I''m delighted by the expressions on the faces of each of the players.  LIke many of your commentors, I am puzzled by the difference in skin tone from front to back.  And I desperately wish there was slightly better focus but with an aperture of 1.6 you got exactly what you exposed for.  I can only think that a higher ISO rating might have made a difference.  But I don''t think I am telling you anything new. But, still, this is an exciting image and it did score well. (I''ll be in Paris towards summer, and know I''ll never find a scene this lovely) IN any event,  I''ll look forward to seeing more of your images on DPC.', '2006-02-05 21:42:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2125030, 280507, 6349, '<td valign="top">sheesh should have done much better</td>', 'sheesh should have done much better', '2006-02-05 21:47:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(2125131, 278040, 6349, '<td valign="top">hahaha.  u did almost as bad as me in the challenge!  :)  some silly stuff going on.  seriously...do you want us to convert our favorites over to this one so you don''t have doubles in your portfolio or what?  Make sure to spread the word.</td>', 'hahaha.  u did almost as bad as me in the challenge!  :)  some silly stuff going on.  seriously...do you want us to convert our favorites over to this one so you don''t have doubles in your portfolio or what?  Make sure to spread the word.', '2006-02-05 22:27:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(2125431, 278359, 42638, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club </b> <br/> <br/>The first thing that strikes me with this picture is the eyes and the emotion emitted from their expressions. There is a magical peace here, The softness in their faces really make me feel very comfortable and brings a smile to my face. <br/> <br/>I really like the way the older gentleman is crouched down more than the younger one, he make an invisible line that moves the viewer from him to the younger one with the brighter expression. The direction of their gaze also continues to lead the viewer. The position of the older mans arm is a great touch. It give me a sense of framing or completeness to that area.<br/> <br/>Like many have said in other comments, it is a bit flat. The grey tones seem to mute both subjects from the background. Increasing the contrast might help here. The younger mans darker hair gives you a good idea of how a more contrast can really power this up. <br/> <br/>The lighting is perfect in my opinion, no harsh areas and the focus is also good. <br/> <br/>Its a very good picture overall and after reading your comment behind the picture, I can see how much more precious this is.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-05 23:44:36.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club   The first thing that strikes me with this picture is the eyes and the emotion emitted from their expressions. There is a magical peace here, The softness in their faces really make me feel very comfortable and brings a smile to my face.  I really like the way the older gentleman is crouched down more than the younger one, he make an invisible line that moves the viewer from him to the younger one with the brighter expression. The direction of their gaze also continues to lead the viewer. The position of the older mans arm is a great touch. It give me a sense of framing or completeness to that area. Like many have said in other comments, it is a bit flat. The grey tones seem to mute both subjects from the background. Increasing the contrast might help here. The younger mans darker hair gives you a good idea of how a more contrast can really power this up.  The lighting is perfect in my opinion, no harsh areas and the focus is also good.  Its a very good picture overall and after reading your comment behind the picture, I can see how much more precious this is.', '2006-02-05 23:43:02.000000', '2006-02-05 23:44:36.000000', 0),
(2125501, 277530, 32001, '<td valign="top">Wow, I got a lot of comments about bad photoshop work. Yes, there are some artifacts from oversharpening around the top of the orange which weren''t noticable on my old crappy monitor, or visible in a print. But the other things commented on were actually part of the original photo. The orange was sitting in a puddle of water, and the edge of the waterline caused an irregular transition that some people saw as bad editing.</td>', 'Wow, I got a lot of comments about bad photoshop work. Yes, there are some artifacts from oversharpening around the top of the orange which weren''t noticable on my old crappy monitor, or visible in a print. But the other things commented on were actually part of the original photo. The orange was sitting in a puddle of water, and the edge of the waterline caused an irregular transition that some people saw as bad editing.', '2006-02-05 23:56:42.000000', NULL, 0);
