(2126003, 276055, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b>A really well captured shot. The image seems to be well exposed and necessary details are visible. Though most of the comments are positive about "snake eye", I can spot some over saturation there...maybe it will be more visible in full size image.<br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b>The warm light adds to the punch. Maybe a little more details would have made it more ideal.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b>Offcourse- Anything would fit in for this challenge theme :)<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b>Oveall a good photograph. I did not voted for this challenge but would have given this something between 6-7 which is inline with average score. You really have got some better captures, of nature, in your profile. <br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Technical:A really well captured shot. The image seems to be well exposed and necessary details are visible. Though most of the comments are positive about "snake eye", I can spot some over saturation there...maybe it will be more visible in full size image. Aesthetic:The warm light adds to the punch. Maybe a little more details would have made it more ideal. Challenge:Offcourse- Anything would fit in for this challenge theme :) Conclusion:Oveall a good photograph. I did not voted for this challenge but would have given this something between 6-7 which is inline with average score. You really have got some better captures, of nature, in your profile.  I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-02-06 02:36:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(2126021, 277237, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b>This is a great capture. Maybe a little bit of tweeking with metering mode might have helped avoiding the "burning" of details on clothes. I like it personally, but I dont think it might be of everyones taste.<br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b>Great! Takes me to past. B/W is ideal choice and the sharp focus on eyes is just right. The "average housewife friend" of yours should not be feeling average anymore...she looks stunning.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b>Offcourse, anything would fit into theme for this challenge :)<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b>Definately a great capture. Maybe a little bit of postprocessing might have improved the overall composition. I think this challenge had many great photos and this one suffered from "relative" syndrome. I''d have given is 7 had I voted for this challenge.<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Technical:This is a great capture. Maybe a little bit of tweeking with metering mode might have helped avoiding the "burning" of details on clothes. I like it personally, but I dont think it might be of everyones taste. Aesthetic:Great! Takes me to past. B/W is ideal choice and the sharp focus on eyes is just right. The "average housewife friend" of yours should not be feeling average anymore...she looks stunning. Challenge:Offcourse, anything would fit into theme for this challenge :) Conclusion:Definately a great capture. Maybe a little bit of postprocessing might have improved the overall composition. I think this challenge had many great photos and this one suffered from "relative" syndrome. I''d have given is 7 had I voted for this challenge. I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-02-06 02:44:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2126023, 281923, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b>This is a nice capture but the focus seems to be little soft. Also the image is a bit "flat" but is well exposed.<br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b>Good emotional feel and b/w is good choice but somehow lacks on overall "photographic" value. This is a great photo for family album though.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b>Offcourse, anything would fit into theme for this challenge :)<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b>Definately a good capture. Maybe a little bit of postprocessing might have improved the overall composition.A little bit more of contrast and sharper focus would have helped.<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-06 02:49:03.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Technical:This is a nice capture but the focus seems to be little soft. Also the image is a bit "flat" but is well exposed. Aesthetic:Good emotional feel and b/w is good choice but somehow lacks on overall "photographic" value. This is a great photo for family album though. Challenge:Offcourse, anything would fit into theme for this challenge :) Conclusion:Definately a good capture. Maybe a little bit of postprocessing might have improved the overall composition.A little bit more of contrast and sharper focus would have helped. I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-02-06 02:44:41.000000', '2006-02-06 02:49:03.000000', 0),
(2126040, 279356, 29269, '<td valign="top">::: Critique Club :::<br/> <br/>Hi<br/> <br/>I am Serge from the Critique Club. The following is a critique for "Crisp Point Lighthouse".<br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> - the most important one: Wonderful photo but - what''s that with the horizon? Could it be the perspective - will check later...<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> Very good. Rule of 3rds applied on both the horizon line and the seashore line. The lighthouse is occupying the middle 3rd of vertical space - everything is pleasing to the eye.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> As this is the best of (free study) it is hard to measure how much does this one meet the challenge. From your perspective (as per your comments) it means a lot to you, so this is your most remarkable (if not the best) photo of 2005.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour, focus, and light):</b> This is another place (other than the horizon that could have been straightened a bit, while leaving the lighthouse perfectly straight) where this photo did not draw all the top scores (although 5.7x is pretty good result). The lighting conditions were not perfect. What is lacking is some contrast between the sea and the sky, and the gray color of the lighthouse against the similarly gray color of the sky behind it. The lighting was very diffused, (sometimes a good thing that eliminates harsh shadows) that in this case subtracted from the photo a little bit. What could have worked is if you dodged the lighthouse to bring out more white in it.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?</b> a big pet peeve at DPC is the crooked horizon. Although it is not a written requirement anywhere to have straight horizons, you WILL lose point(s) on it. That''s one thing guaranteed to bring your score up.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> Overall nice and appealing photo. Among 800+ entries in the best of 2005 (and I voted on all of them) I actually remembered this one. And that is something to take with you - it is a memorable photo after all.<br/> <br/>if you have any questions on the critique feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Best regards,<br/> <br/>-Serge</td>', '::: Critique Club ::: Hi I am Serge from the Critique Club. The following is a critique for "Crisp Point Lighthouse". First Impression - the most important one: Wonderful photo but - what''s that with the horizon? Could it be the perspective - will check later... Composition: Very good. Rule of 3rds applied on both the horizon line and the seashore line. The lighthouse is occupying the middle 3rd of vertical space - everything is pleasing to the eye. Subject: As this is the best of (free study) it is hard to measure how much does this one meet the challenge. From your perspective (as per your comments) it means a lot to you, so this is your most remarkable (if not the best) photo of 2005. Technical (Colour, focus, and light): This is another place (other than the horizon that could have been straightened a bit, while leaving the lighthouse perfectly straight) where this photo did not draw all the top scores (although 5.7x is pretty good result). The lighting conditions were not perfect. What is lacking is some contrast between the sea and the sky, and the gray color of the lighthouse against the similarly gray color of the sky behind it. The lighting was very diffused, (sometimes a good thing that eliminates harsh shadows) that in this case subtracted from the photo a little bit. What could have worked is if you dodged the lighthouse to bring out more white in it. To grow its vote? a big pet peeve at DPC is the crooked horizon. Although it is not a written requirement anywhere to have straight horizons, you WILL lose point(s) on it. That''s one thing guaranteed to bring your score up. Summary: Overall nice and appealing photo. Among 800+ entries in the best of 2005 (and I voted on all of them) I actually remembered this one. And that is something to take with you - it is a memorable photo after all. if you have any questions on the critique feel free to PM me. Best regards, -Serge', '2006-02-06 03:06:09.000000', NULL, 0),
